{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":15,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ telephone telephones) (+ \"greeted with skepticism\" \"met with skepticism\" \"initial skepticism\" \"public skepticism\" \"viewed with suspicion\") (+ invention invented bell))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ telephone telephones) (+ \"greeted with skepticism\" \"met with skepticism\" \"initial skepticism\" \"public skepticism\" \"viewed with suspicion\") (+ invention invented bell))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_1644967610#5_1848040311","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1751","rank":1,"snippet":"was the first to receive FCC approval in 1948. He sold more than 100 thousand units of his system, mostly to farmers and the U.S. Coast Guard. Two-way radios Cartoonist Chester Gould asked if he could use Gross' concept of a miniaturized two-way radio in his Dick Tracy comic strip. The result was the Dick Tracy two-way wrist radio. Telephone pager Another breakthrough came in 1949 when he adapted his two-way radios to one-way for cordless remote telephonic signaling. He had effectively <cover>invented the first telephone pager system. His intention for this system was to be used by medical doctors, but was met with skepticism </cover>by doctors who were afraid the system would upset patients. This same technology is used in one-way radio signaling devices such as garage door openers. Later years In 1950 he tried in vain to interest telephone companies in mobile telephony. Bell Telephone was uninterested, and other companies were afraid of Bell's monopoly on transmission lines.\" , \"start_char\": 3431.000000 , \"title\": \"Al Gross (engineer) - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/"},"time":290},"model_calls":15,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
