{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":31,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ \"viewed it with suspicion\" \"suspected a diabolical origin\" \"instrument of the devil\" \"wires would attract lightning\" \"pulled down\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ \"viewed it with suspicion\" \"suspected a diabolical origin\" \"instrument of the devil\" \"wires would attract lightning\" \"pulled down\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2078195365#10_3134544323","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1765","rank":1,"snippet":"Instruments 8 Socialism 7 Smoking 6 The Telephone 5 Dice 4 The Smurfs 3 The Cross 2 Soccer 1 Theater \" , \"segment\": \"6 The Telephone Writer Ambrose Bierce once defined the telephone as “an invention of the devil which abrogates some of the advantages of making a disagreeable person keep his distance.” He was being satirical, but when the telephone was invented, there were many who viewed it with suspicion and suspected a diabolical origin. When the telephone was first introduced in Sweden, many rural folks feared it was magical and that <cover>telephone wires would attract lightning </cover>or evil spirits. Preachers outright decried the telephone as an instrument of the Devil, and lines were often pulled down or sabotaged by irate farmers. The Amish and Old Order Mennonites of Pennsylvania had a similar view. Their societies were informed by gelassenheit, a sense of belonging and separation from the outside world driven by dress, language, tradition, nonverbal communication, and a moral code. New forms of communication were perceived as a threat to face-to-face communication, which enforced the social order. One Mennonite reported an elder gravely warning, “There goes the devil’s wires .” Ethiopian journalist ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2078195365#10_3134544323","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":47170},"model_calls":31,"document_judgments":27,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
