{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":39,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ \"slow to catch on\" \"slow adoption\" \"customers were scarce\" \"initial excitement waned\" \"little interest\") (+ 1876 bell invented))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ \"slow to catch on\" \"slow adoption\" \"customers were scarce\" \"initial excitement waned\" \"little interest\") (+ 1876 bell invented))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_651027006#12_625728673","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1771","rank":1,"snippet":"\": \"Trying to make some quick cash, Bell offered Western Union the patent rights to the telephone for $100,000. The telegraph company had a nationwide network of wires in place and could naturally have branched out. Content with its telegraph monopoly, Western Union turned down Bell’s offer and lost the chance to monopolize another lucrative industry. By the summer of 1877, the telephone had become a business. The first private lines, which typically connected a businessman’s home and his office, had been placed in service. The first commercial <cover>telephone switchboard opened the following year in New Haven. Bell had little interest </cover>in being a businessman. In July 1877, he married Mabel Hubbard, and set out for what proved a long honeymoon in England. He left the growing Bell Telephone Company to Hubbard and Sanders, and went on to a long and productive career as an independent researcher and inventor. In 1880, he invented and patented the photophone, which transmitted voices over beams of light.\" , \"start_char\": 7017.000000 , \"title\": \"Alexander Graham Bell - Engineering and Technology History Wiki\" , \"url\":"},"time":7320},"model_calls":39,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":11,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
