{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":54,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bell had tremendous difficulty selling his invention\" telephone)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bell had tremendous difficulty selling his invention\" telephone)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_171002242#6_390936035","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1783","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_171002242#6_390936035\" , \"end_char\": 5997.000000 , \"headings\": \"TELEPHONY (VOICE TELECOMMUNICATIONS) TELEPHONY (VOICE TELECOMMUNICATIONS) HISTORICAL DEVELOPMENT OF TELEPHONY RECENT TELECOMMUNICATIONS REFORM<cover>TELEPHONE SYSTEMS AND COMPONENTS TRENDS IN TELEPHONY BUSINESS APPLICATIONS OF VOICE COMMUNICATION S FURTHER READING: User Contributions: \" , \"segment\": \"But Bell had tremendous difficulty selling his invention. </cover>He failed to  interest Western Union, which maintained that there was no use for such a  toy when key operators could communicate faster than people could talk. To  raise money, Bell demonstrated his invention in sideshows, and eventually  raised enough to set up the Bell Telephone Company with networks in Boston  and New York, and even acquired Gray's rival Western Electric  Company. As the telephone grew in popularity, it became obvious that Western Union  had failed to appreciate the greatest asset of the telephone: anyone, not just skilled key operators, could use it. With the prospect of  serving a thousand times as many accounts, Western Union scrambled to get  in on the business and hired Thomas Edison (1847-1931) to develop a  superior ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_171002242#6_390936035","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":770},"model_calls":54,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
