{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":54,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1783"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1783"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_171002242#5_390934260","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1783","rank":2,"snippet":"in a  laboratory down the hallway. When Watson arrived, he reported that he had  heard Bell's cry over the wires. The two worked diligently to prepare a patent application, but in the  oddest coincidence, filed their patent the very same day as Elisha Gray (1835-1901), another inventor who had  developed a similar but slightly superior device. After an acrimonious  17-year legal battle, Bell was awarded the patent in 1876. But <cover>Bell had tremendous difficulty selling his invention. He failed to  interest Western Union, which maintained that there was no use for such a  toy when key operators could communicate faster than people could talk. To  raise money, Bell demonstrated his invention in sideshows, and eventually  raised enough to set up the Bell Telephone </cover>Company with networks in Boston  and New York, and even acquired Gray's rival Western Electric  Company. As the telephone grew in popularity, it became obvious that Western Union  had failed to appreciate the greatest asset of the telephone: anyone, not just skilled key operators, could use it.\" , \"start_char\": 4039.000000 , \"title\": \"Telephony (Voice Telecommunications)\" , \"url\":","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_171002242#5_390934260","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":54,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
