{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"telephone\" (+ \"customers were scarce\" \"looked as if it wouldn't last\" \"wouldn't last the decade\" \"initial excitement\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"telephone\" (+ \"customers were scarce\" \"looked as if it wouldn't last\" \"wouldn't last the decade\" \"initial excitement\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#7_1925541721","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1784","rank":1,"snippet":"Works How AT&T Works The Beginning Theodore Vail One Policy, One System: Universal Service What AT&T Did (and Still Does) Transmission Switching Management The End of Monopoly After the Breakup SBC: Ma Bell's Heir Apparent? \" , \"segment\": \"the Bell telephone. At first the telephone got quite a bit of attention as a technological wonder, debuting at the Philadelphia Centennial Exhibition on July 25, 1876. But the initial excitement waned and customers were scarce. Bell returned to Scotland and left the business operations to Hubbard. Without visionary leadership, Bell <cover>Telephone looked as if it wouldn't last </cover>the decade, much less conquer the next century. Hubbard offered the company to William Orton, then president of Western Union, for $100,000. Orton passed, dismissing the telephone as an electrical toy. Companies like AT&T stick around for so long because they have great products and know how to treat their customers. Click here to learn all about customer relations. Advertisement Contents Theodore Vail One Policy, One System:\" , \"start_char\": 3249.000000 , \"title\": \"How AT&T Works | HowStuffWorks\" , \"url\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#7_1925541721","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":22230},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1784"},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-19025","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1784"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1784"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#6_1925540477","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1784","rank":2,"snippet":"Service What AT&T Did (and Still Does) Transmission Switching Management The End of Monopoly After the Breakup SBC: Ma Bell's Heir Apparent? \" , \"segment\": \"Bell spilled liquid on his desk and said reflexively, \\\"Watson come here, I need you.\\\" Watson wasn't within earshot, but he heard the request just the same. He received the call from the telephone. A patent attorney, Hubbard had already filed patents in Bell's name for the device. Now he and Sanders formed a company to sell this new invention to the world: the Bell telephone. At first the telephone got quite a bit of attention as a technological wonder, debuting at the Philadelphia Centennial Exhibition on July 25, 1876. But the initial excitement waned and customers were scarce. Bell returned to Scotland and left the business operations to Hubbard. Without visionary leadership, Bell <cover>Telephone looked as if it wouldn't last </cover>the decade, much less conquer the next century.\" , \"start_char\": 2891.000000 , \"title\": \"How AT&T Works | HowStuffWorks\" , \"url\": \"https://money.howstuffworks.com/att.htm\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#6_1925540477","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
